WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.560
When compared to time travel and&nbsp;
the concept of free will, the giant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.540
humanoid rabbit in Donnie Darko is one of&nbsp;
the film's more straightforward plot points.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.680
Although it has a lot to offer&nbsp;
beyond just rational explanations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.660
it's impossible to comprehend Donnie Darko&nbsp;
without a basic understanding of its science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.680
fiction elements. Fortunately, Roberta&nbsp;
"Grandma Death" Sparrow wrote her book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.940
The Philosophy of Time Travel, on&nbsp;
this very subject. At various points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.660
Donnie and his teacher, Professor&nbsp;
Monnitoff paraphrase excerpts for us.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.120
"Every living thing follows a long-set path,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.580
and if you could see your path or channel,&nbsp;
then you could see into the future right?"

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.300
In short, the appearance of the paradoxical&nbsp;
jet engine at the beginning of the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.260
creates a "Tangent Universe." As the&nbsp;
nearest person to "The Artifact,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.820
Donnie becomes charged with the mission&nbsp;
to ensure the plane enters the vortex at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.180
the end of the film and "returns"&nbsp;
the engine to where it came from.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:59.280
In other words, Donnie Darko is set in a&nbsp;
parallel universe where everyone in Donnie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:05.340
life is unconsciously working to resolve this jet&nbsp;
engine quandary. Everything from Donnie's troubled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:10.560
dreams to his increased strength that enables him&nbsp;
to put an axe into a statue's head to his visions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.480
of a dead Frank in the rabbit costume — they're&nbsp;
all part of the mythology laid out in the book.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.980
The presence of the engine will eventually&nbsp;
cause a black hole in the "Primary Universe,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:25.140
which is essentially the regular timeline. For&nbsp;
this reason, Frank isn't lying when he gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:30.480
Donnie the precise time that the world will end.&nbsp;
The curious can find excerpts online from The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:35.801
Philosophy of Time Travel and see how thoroughly&nbsp;
it accounts for each little bit of Donnie Darko.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:41.520
As explained in Sparrow's philosophy, Frank is&nbsp;
a member of "The Manipulated Dead" because he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.960
dies during the alternate timeline. His role is&nbsp;
to be Donnie's guide through his adventure with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:51.480
the jet engine. He wakes him up and serves as&nbsp;
the unsettling Virgil to Donnie's teenage Dante.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:56.100
However, if you view the movie through the lens&nbsp;
of Donnie's potential paranoid schizophrenia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:01.500
there are a few clues that the messenger might&nbsp;
live solely in his mind. We eventually learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.840
that Frank is Donnie's sister's boyfriend, so&nbsp;
it's possible Donnie is drawing from reality.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.760
Midway through the movie, we see that Donnie has&nbsp;
also put a drawing of Frank in the rabbit costume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.800
over his wall calendar. It's not clear if Donnie&nbsp;
drew this himself, and in fact we learn at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.600
end of the movie that Frank is an artist when we&nbsp;
see his early sketches of the rabbit mask design.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.520
Did he draw Donnie's picture as well?&nbsp;
Interpretations range from Frank being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.240
a product of Donnie's psychosis to the&nbsp;
entire story being something that Frank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.840
himself is remembering as a dream when we&nbsp;
see him touch his eye near the movie's end.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:39.420
Graham Greene's short story The Destructors&nbsp;
is arguably just as important to understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:44.880
Donnie Darko as the time travel manual. As we come&nbsp;
to learn, Donnie has lived a life that parallels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:50.220
that of the boys in the story who destroy an old&nbsp;
man's home; Donnie is currently in therapy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.080
can't drive until he's 21 after "accidentally"&nbsp;
burning down an abandoned house himself.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.460
Generally morose and inarticulate&nbsp;
about his problems, he immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.980
understands the motivations&nbsp;
of the kids in the story:

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.600
"Destruction is a form of creation, so the&nbsp;
fact that they burned the money is ironic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.080
They just wanted to see what happens&nbsp;
when they tear the world apart."

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:15.420
This makes Donnie a perfect vessel for Frank,&nbsp;
who directs him to flood the school and burn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:20.820
down Jim Cunningham's house. However, all of his&nbsp;
destructive actions in the movie ultimately serve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:25.320
to create the world anew and restore order&nbsp;
by closing the loop with the jet engine.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:29.700
In a broad and operatic sense,&nbsp;
Donnie Darko is an allegory for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:33.900
what Graham Green describes as "the pain&nbsp;
of puberty." When you become a teenager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:39.960
you enter a phase of nihilistic destruction —&nbsp;
whether it's a goth-punk phase or more aimless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:43.620
rebellion against the status quo. In&nbsp;
destroying your view of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:49.080
you find an entirely new one, which you've created&nbsp;
for yourself in the throes of early maturation.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:55.740
A big part of growing up is the acceptance&nbsp;
of complexity: There's no real good and evil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:01.500
and the world isn't as simple as childhood&nbsp;
often makes it seem. This is why Donnie sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.700
so thoroughly and immediately through&nbsp;
Jim Cunningham's self-help schtick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.200
which involves reducing every scenario&nbsp;
in life to either "fear" or "love."

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:15.120
Without boiling its message down, Donnie Darko&nbsp;
states its purpose with the story of Ling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.320
Ling and the wallet, in which a woman returns&nbsp;
a lost wallet but not the money inside. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:24.720
Donnie's teacher insists that the scenario be&nbsp;
analyzed on a simple spectrum of fear or love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.880
Donnie insists that not everything can be&nbsp;
categorized or reduced to a simple explanation.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:34.980
Our protagonist is outraged by the fear-love&nbsp;
spectrum for the same reasons he is drawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.100
to Gretchen's emotional honesty&nbsp;
about her messed-up family life:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:43.080
he realizes the full realm of human&nbsp;
emotion is available to him. The film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.660
makes it clear that if you spend&nbsp;
your life looking for clarity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:51.180
you'll end up a cardboard cut-out of a person&nbsp;
like Cunningham or the dreadful Mrs. Farmer.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:54.840
Despite having seemingly unlocked&nbsp;
the secrets of the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:59.700
Roberta Sparrow lives up to her "Grandma Death"&nbsp;
nickname when she encounters Donnie and points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:04.620
out that everything dies alone. One could&nbsp;
argue that, being one of the "Manipulated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:08.340
Living" in the Tangent Universe according&nbsp;
to her own theory, Roberta shares this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:12.600
insight with Donnie so he comes to accept&nbsp;
the sacrifice he ultimately has to make.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:17.760
As we learn in Donnie's therapy sessions, the idea&nbsp;
that everyone dies alone is something he's tired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.920
of debating internally; it makes the search for&nbsp;
meaning or a higher power fundamentally absurd.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:28.320
As we age into adults, we struggle to truly&nbsp;
reckon with the idea of mortality. Donnie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.520
struggles with this concept, especially&nbsp;
as he begins to realize he has to die to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:35.640
stop the world from ending. But&nbsp;
unlike his childhood dog Tally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:40.320
he doesn't crawl off to be alone.&nbsp;
Instead, he returns to his family home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:45.840
dying to save his parents, sisters, Gretchen,&nbsp;
and everyone else he cares about the most.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:50.760
The existential despair at the idea of&nbsp;
dying alone is only troubling insofar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:55.800
as human connection gives life meaning.&nbsp;
Even in her madness and decades of fear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.040
driven by glimpses of the true&nbsp;
nature of time and the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.939
Roberta Sparrow still checks her mail&nbsp;
every day, hoping for someone to reach out.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:09.420
The "spears," or vectors emanating from&nbsp;
people's chests that Donnie begins to see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:14.460
are a way for the movie to address an inevitable&nbsp;
paradox that any time travel flick runs into:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:19.380
free will. If you can see your future,&nbsp;
it stands to reason that you would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:24.240
the option to change it. Donnie follows his own&nbsp;
spear to the gun hidden in his parents closet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.780
but it's not until much later that we&nbsp;
learn he chooses to take it with him.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:33.660
Later, looking into Gretchen's spear, he seems to&nbsp;
get a whole dose of her future in a flash, and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:39.540
he rushes to Roberta Sparrow's house. Donnie seems&nbsp;
content to operate "within God's channel" — as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:44.100
puts it to Dr. Monnitoff — and follow his&nbsp;
glimpses of the future wherever they lead.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:48.900
He does seem surprised when the journey&nbsp;
to Roberta Sparrow's cellar inadvertently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:52.980
leads to Gretchen's death because he shoots&nbsp;
Frank in retaliation with his parents' gun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:57.480
His motivations are as unclear as the&nbsp;
paradox of free will in the abstract:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:03.060
are we the architects of our own fate, or are&nbsp;
we just following a path that we can't see?

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:08.700
Donnie's English teacher, Ms. Pomeroy plays a&nbsp;
small but key role in the climactic events of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:13.140
the movie, as the phrase "cellar door" leads&nbsp;
Donnie and Gretchen to their fateful encounter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:18.000
with the school bullies. Somewhat randomly,&nbsp;
Ms. Pomeroy writes the words on her chalkboard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:23.820
inspired by a, quote, "famous linguist" who&nbsp;
claimed it was the most beautiful phrase in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:29.580
the English language. That famous linguist was, in&nbsp;
fact, The Lord of the Rings author J.R.R. Tolkien.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:33.960
It's a relatively absurd connection,&nbsp;
and the artificiality of it is driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:37.980
home explicitly when Donnie mumbles&nbsp;
a certain phrase while fighting:

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:40.962
"Deus ex machina."

00:07:40.962 --> 00:07:44.520
"Deus ex machina" refers to a random&nbsp;
and unlikely act occurring in a story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:50.160
usually to save the protagonist from certain&nbsp;
peril. Donnie seems at least subconsciously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.720
aware that the entire last month of his&nbsp;
life is one endless "deus ex machina."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:08:00.151
All of these unlikely events are conspiring&nbsp;
to achieve a certain world-saving result.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:05.700
The reason Frank has Donnie burn down Jim&nbsp;
Cunningham's house is one of the more difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:10.680
things to parse in Donnie Darko. According to&nbsp;
Roberta Sparrow, Frank is setting an "Ensurance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:16.380
Trap" to make sure that Donnie goes through with&nbsp;
his self-sacrifice. Since Mrs. Farmer ends up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:21.120
spearheading Cunningham's defense efforts, she&nbsp;
can't accompany the dance team Sparkle Motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:26.520
when they get on Star Search in Los Angeles. For&nbsp;
this reason, Donnie's mother, Rose, goes instead.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:32.220
As an indirect result of all this switching,&nbsp;
the team takes a red-eye flight back instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:37.620
of waiting until the next day, so this ensures&nbsp;
that Donnie's mother and younger sister will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:42.900
be on the very plane that loses an engine. In&nbsp;
addition to preventing Gretchen's death, Donnie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:48.420
must also reset the timeline to save his mother&nbsp;
and sister. This hits a little harder, since he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:52.560
only got to know Gretchen as a result of Frank's&nbsp;
instruction to flood the school to begin with.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.600
Shortly before the dramatic events of&nbsp;
Halloween night, Donnie writes Roberta Sparrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:02.580
a long-awaited letter. Even though he marks&nbsp;
the envelope "EXTREMELY IMPORTANT," the letter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:07.860
doesn't really seem all that time-sensitive. He&nbsp;
says cryptically that he has lots of questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:13.860
for her that he's afraid to know the answers to.&nbsp;
The letter ends with a morbidly optimistic phrase.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:16.560
"I hope that when the world comes to an end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:23.640
I can breathe a sigh of relief, because&nbsp;
there will be so much to look forward to."

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:29.100
This observation indicates that he's come&nbsp;
to accept his death and is perhaps aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:33.000
that it saves the world in a way. But&nbsp;
it's also a deep message about teenage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.680
angst and existentialism. In the painful&nbsp;
and confusing throes of late adolescence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:41.880
every problem seems to have&nbsp;
intolerable life-and-death stakes.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:45.480
Once you admit to yourself that the&nbsp;
things you fear won't end the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:51.600
you can live freely and be yourself in a reality&nbsp;
that makes no sense. In dying to save the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.400
Donnie is facing the unknown, just&nbsp;
as every child grows up and has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.780
define the world for themselves. In its&nbsp;
macabre meditation on fate and death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:06.720
Donnie Darko arrives at a hopeful place: every&nbsp;
day is the first day of the rest of your life.

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.880
It's not particularly clear why Donnie drives&nbsp;
up to the mountains with Gretchen's body near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:15.420
the very end of the movie, though it&nbsp;
kind of brings the story full circle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:20.100
It's possible that what we've seen in Donnie&nbsp;
Darko is just one iteration of a time loop:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:24.240
the jet engine creates the unstable pocket&nbsp;
universe, and everyone within it repeats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.560
the same span of time until they successfully&nbsp;
transport the engine back to where it arrived.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:34.800
This would explain why Donnie is already waking&nbsp;
up after "sleepwalking" at night when we first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.720
see him: he's at the beginning of another&nbsp;
attempt. When it finally ends, he watches the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:45.300
plane enter the vortex with a bemused look; it's&nbsp;
possible he's actually causing the plane to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:49.980
caught in the breach via telekinesis. Another&nbsp;
explanation would be that he needs to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:54.780
that height to travel back through the wormhole&nbsp;
himself to return to his bedroom on October 2.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:56.820
With the timeline restored,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.820 --> 00:11:01.260
Donnie Darko shows that all of the major&nbsp;
characters Donnie encountered throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:07.440
story are unable to sleep on October 2, clearly&nbsp;
haunted by "memories" of an alternate timeline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:13.920
that will now never unfold. Knowing that our&nbsp;
hero has just died, we reflect on how many people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:19.860
his life actually touched even though he was&nbsp;
generally so self-possessed with his own issues.

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:25.320
Haunted by these glimpses, Gretchen and Donnie's&nbsp;
mother share a strange wave at the very end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:30.780
the film: a mother whose world has just been&nbsp;
shattered, waving to a young woman from a broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:36.300
home just beginning to put her own world back&nbsp;
together as a young adult. Donnie Darko is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:43.560
the terrifying, complex, and hopeful intersection&nbsp;
between fear and love. It's hard to decide if it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:48.360
scarier to consider the world ending, or to think&nbsp;
about it carrying on without us when we're gone.

